{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>Pass before the people</b> — like the expression \"he moved the people to the cities\" (Genesis 47:21), \"and I will make your enemies pass to a land you do not know\" (Jeremiah 15:14), meaning that you pass from them to another, or like \"and he passed the Cushite\" (II Samuel 18:23), \"and he passed before them\" (Genesis 33:3), meaning that he went first ahead of them. The sense of this verse is that the people were in Rephidim, while the rock from which the waters came was in Horeb — that is, Mount Sinai — according to the opinion of the early authorities (see tractate Shabbat 89a), or a city before the mountain close to it, in my opinion, as I will yet explain (below 19:1; Deuteronomy 1:6). Now Moses needed to go ahead of the people to pass from Rephidim to Horeb, a distance of a few parasangs, ahead of them from the camp, and therefore He said to him: <b>Pass before the people, and take with you from the elders of Israel, and you shall go</b> — meaning, you shall go until you see Me standing before you upon the rock in Horeb.\nNow he struck the rock and waters came forth from it, and Scripture did not state \"and the congregation drank and their livestock\" as in the second instance (Numbers 20:11). But it is known that they did so, and it is clear that the people did not go to Horeb to drink, for they did not arrive before Mount Sinai until afterward, in the third month. Rather, they sent their young men and their livestock there to draw water and bring it to them, as is the custom of camps. And it seems likely to me that waters came forth from the rock in Horeb — cold, flowing waters — and went to Rephidim, and there they drank them; and that is what Scripture says (Psalms 78:16): \"He brought streams from the rock and caused waters to flow down like rivers,\" and it is written (Psalms 105:41): \"He opened the rock and waters gushed out; they went in the dry places like a river.\" And what it says (Psalms 78:20): \"Behold, He struck the rock and waters gushed out, and streams overflowed\" — that too concerns this rock in Horeb, according to the plain sense. But the second rock, which was in Kadesh, was split open in it like a springing well of waters; therefore it says (Numbers 21:16): \"this is the well of which YHWH spoke to Moses,\" and they said in the Song (Numbers 21:18): \"the well which the princes dug\" — for it was like a dug well. Therefore it says there (20:11): <b>and the congregation drank and their livestock</b> — that they drank immediately there in that place. But here, rivers flowed from it and they drank in their tents at their pleasure. And even though all was through Miriam's well, according to the tradition of our Rabbis (Numbers Rabbah 19:25), it is possible that the first time and all forty years it drew water like rivers, and the second time, because of the punishment that occurred there, it was made like a dug well full of living waters." "The sense of <b>and your staff with which you struck</b> — is in the imperative addressed to Aaron. He mentioned in it the plague of the Nile, and did not say \"the staff which turned into a serpent\" or \"the staff with which you performed the signs,\" to mention in it a wonder — for then He turned the waters into blood and removed them from their nature, and now He will bring water from the flinty rock, and behold, He will perform in it a thing and its opposite."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {0 "The citation 'below 19:1; Deuteronomy 1:6' renders Ramban's Hebrew abbreviation להלן יט א, דברים א ו."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:09:54.742209338Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}